\begin_layout Section
MPC with infinite horizon
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Deriving the Euler equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Bellman equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The (representative) consumer faces the following utility maximization problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\max_{c_{t},a_{t+1}\forall t}U_{t} & \equiv\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}\beta^{t+k}\frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t+k}}{1-\sigma}\\
s.t.\\
c_{t}+a_{t+1} & =y_{t}+(1+r)a_{t}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\left(\prod^{T}_{t=0}(1+r)^{-1}\right)a_{T+1}\geq0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}$
\end_inset

 is given and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$
\end_inset

.
 First I derive the Bellman equation explicitely (for an arbitrary starting index 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 which will usually be set to 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
V(a_{t}) & \equiv\max_{\{c_{t+k},a_{t+1+k}\}^{\infty}_{k=0}}\underbrace{\left\{ \sum^{\infty}_{k=0}\beta^{k}\frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t+k}}{1-\sigma}\right\} }_{U_{t}}\\
 & =\max_{\{c_{t+k},a_{t+1+k}\}^{\infty}_{k=0}}\left\{ \frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t}}{1-\sigma}+\beta\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}\beta^{k}\frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t+1+k}}{1-\sigma}\right\} \\
 & =\max_{c_{t},a_{t+1}}\left\{ \frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t}}{1-\sigma}+\beta V(a_{t+1})\right\} \\
 & =\max_{a_{t+1}}\left\{ \frac{\left(y_{t}+(1+r)a_{t}-a_{t+1}\right)^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}+\beta V(a_{t+1})\right\} .
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first order condition for an internal optima (for the right hand side) is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{\left(y_{t}+(1+r)a_{t}-a_{t+1}\right)^{-\sigma}}_{c_{t}}=\beta V_{a}(a_{t+1}),\label{eq:foc}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where we have defined 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{a}(a_{t})\equiv\frac{\partial V}{\partial a_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 By the envelope theorem (assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is differentiable) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{a}(a_{t+1})=(1+r)c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}.\label{eq:envelope}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
Proof of envelope theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Define the optimal asset policy 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{*}_{t+1}=g(a_{t})$
\end_inset

 as the function that solves the Bellman equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V(a_{t})=u\left(y_{t}+(1+r)a_{t}-g(a_{t})\right)+\beta V\left(g(a_{t})\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating wrt.
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
V_{a}(a_{t}) & =\underbrace{u'(c_{t})\left((1+r)-g'(a_{t})\right)}_{\text{gain due to increased current consumption}}+\underbrace{\beta V_{a}\left(g(a_{t})\right)g'(a_{t})}_{\text{gain due to increased savings}}\\
 & =(1+r)u'(c_{t})+g'(a)\underbrace{\left(\beta V_{a}\left(g(a_{t})\right)-u'(c_{t})\right)}_{=0\text{ (because of FOC)}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intuitively 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\beta V_{a}\left(g(a_{t})\right)-u'(c_{t})\right)=0$
\end_inset

 because the margianl gain of spending is equal the marginal gain of saving at the maximum.
 therefore shifting this a little bit does not matter for the value function.
 So basically the increase in assets is distributed as utility gain due to increased current consumption and increased future savings,
 but disregarding the distribution over current and future consumption as this gives the same marginal utility at the maximum,
 which is 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+r)u'(c_{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Euler equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Combining 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:foc}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:envelope}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 we obtain the well known Euler equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c^{-\sigma}_{t}=\beta(1+r)c^{-\sigma}_{t+1},\label{eq:euler}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is a nessesary condition for an internal optimal consumption path.
 This has a very nice intepretation:
 The marginal utility today (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{-\sigma}_{t}$
\end_inset

) should equal the marginal utility of saving a little bit and spending it tomorrow (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{\ensuremath{\beta(1+r)c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}}) from todays perspective}$
\end_inset

.
 If that was not the case then the household could shift consumption and increase total utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Transversality condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the maximization problem we have the no ponzi-scheme condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\left(\prod^{T}_{t=0}(1+r)^{-1}\right)a_{T+1}=\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\left((1+r)^{-(T+1)}\right)a_{T+1}\geq0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the utility function is monotone in consumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial U_{t}}{\partial c_{t+k}}>0,\quad\forall k\geq0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the consumer gains no direct utility from assets
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial U_{t}}{\partial a_{t+k}}=0,\quad\forall k\geq0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we conclude that the consumer would not increase savings indefinitely,
 and such the transversality condition is born
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}\left((1+r)^{-(T+1)}\right)a_{T+1}=0.\label{eq:transversality}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Iterating forward the budget constraint yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
a_{t} & =(1+r)^{-1}\left(c_{t}-y_{t}\right)+(1+r)^{-1}a_{t+1}\\
 & =(1+r)^{-1}\left(c_{t}-y_{t}\right)+(1+r)^{-1}\left((1+r)^{-1}\left(c_{t+1}-y_{t+1}\right)+(1+r)^{-1}a_{t+2}\right)\\
 & =(1+r)^{-1}\left(c_{t}-y_{t}\right)+(1+r)^{-2}\left(c_{t+1}-y_{t+1}\right)+(1+r)^{-2}a_{t+2}\\
 & =(1+r)^{-1}\left(c_{t}-y_{t}\right)+(1+r)^{-1}+(1+r)^{-2}\left(c_{t+1}-y_{t+1}\right)\\
 & \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad+(1+r)^{-3}\left(c_{t+2}-y_{t+2}\right)+(1+r)^{-3}a_{t+3}\\
 & =\sum^{T}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-(k+1)}\left(c_{t+k}-y_{t+k}\right)+(1+r)^{-(T+1)}a_{T+1}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Taking the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $T\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

,
 evaluating at the starting period 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 and using 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:transversality}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
a_{0} & =\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-(k+1)}\left(c_{k}-y_{k}\right)+\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}(1+r)^{-(T+1)}a_{T+1}\\
 & =\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-(k+1)}\left(c_{k}-y_{k}\right)\iff
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-k}c_{k}=(1+r)a_{0}+h_{0},\label{eq:budget}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{0}\equiv\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-k}y_{k}$
\end_inset

 is the present value of human capital / income stream.
 That is the present value of consumption is equal to the value of period-0 assets and the present value of income.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Consumption function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly we rewrite the Euler equation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:euler}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c^{-\sigma}_{t}=\beta(1+r)c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}\iff c_{t}=\left(\beta(1+r)\right)^{-1/\sigma}c_{t+1},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and derive the consumption at every period in terms of initial consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
c_{t} & =\left(\beta(1+r)\right)^{1/\sigma}c_{t-1}=\left(\beta(1+r)\right)^{t/\sigma}c_{0}.\label{eq:gabbah}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:gabbah}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 into the budget constraint 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:budget}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 yields the consumption function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left[\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}\left(\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)^{k}\right]c_{0} & =\left(1-\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)^{-1}c_{0}=(1+r)a_{0}+h_{0}\iff
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{0}=\left(1-\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)\left((1+r)a_{0}+h_{0}\right).\label{eq:confunc}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confunc}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is the consumption function describing how consumption today (e.g.
 period 0) depends on current assets (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+r)a_{0}$
\end_inset

) and the total discounted income stream (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{0}\equiv\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}(1+r)^{-k}y_{k}$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(1+r)=1$
\end_inset

 the Euler equation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:euler}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c^{-\sigma}_{t}=c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}\iff c_{t}=c_{t+1}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 stable consumption across all periods.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(1+r)=1$
\end_inset

 we have that discounting due to delayed consumption is exactely offset by increased consumption in the next period due to the rent recieved by saving.
 I think the assignment wants me to anwser PIH (the permanent income hypothesis) but I do not agree.
 PIH refers to households making decisions based on their total future discounted cashflows.
 This is captured in 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confunc}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 but does not directly imply constant consumption as far as I understand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Marginal Propensities to Consume (MPCs)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confunc}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 we calculate the MPCs of the model.
 Firstly the MPC of a 
\emph on
one-of 
\emph default
or 
\emph on
windfall 
\emph default
income increase in any given period 
\begin_inset Formula $t=k$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial y_{k}}=\left(1-\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)\left(\frac{1}{1+r}\right)^{k},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and secondly a permant income increase (i.e.
 where income in each period increases by the same amount)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial\Delta y}\equiv\sum^{\infty}_{k=0}\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial y_{k}}=\left(1-\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)\frac{1}{1-(1+r)^{-1}}=\left(1-\frac{\left(\beta(1+r\right)^{1/\sigma}}{1+r}\right)\frac{1+r}{r}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta(1+r)=1$
\end_inset

 where the Euler equation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:euler}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 implies that consumption is constant (as we saw before) we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial\Delta y}=1$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the provided calibration we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial y_{0}}=1-\frac{\left(0.95(1+0.04\right)^{1/2}}{1+0.04}\approx0.044,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial c_{0}}{\partial\Delta y}=\left(1-\frac{\left(0.95(1+0.04\right)^{1/2}}{1+0.04}\right)\frac{1+0.04}{0.04}\approx1.150.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting MPCs are lower than any conventional empirical estimate and therefore the model is plausably wrong in some fundamental way.
 One very economics coated argument could be that people are credit constrained in the real world.
 This would make the MPCs for credit constrained people larger because they are below their unconstrained optimal consumption path.
 Another classic explanation has to do with mortality risk:
 people are always at risk of dying and if they dont fully care about their bequest as strongly as their own lived life,
 then MPC out of current income will rise.
 Simply because future states are discounted more heavily.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
My own speculation would be that people do not maximise in this way where they take the whole future path into account and just consume for the fun of it.
 Evidence of this can be found in credit card transaction data where there are HUGE spikes around pay-day which would be hard to fit in a framework where households have diminishing marginal utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Ramsey model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The firm maximises profit in each period (non-dynamically) subject to a Cobb-Douglas production technology.
 Formally it solves:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{K_{t},L_{t}}\left\{ \pi(K_{t},L_{t})=AK^{\alpha}_{t}L^{1-\alpha}_{t}-w_{t}L_{t}-r_{t}K_{t}\right\} ,\label{eq:firmmax}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 The problem 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:firmmax}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 has the following nessesary first order conditions for an internal optimum
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\pi}{\partial K_{t}}=A\alpha K^{\alpha-1}_{t}L^{1-\alpha}_{t}-r_{t}=0\iff\alpha Ak^{\alpha-1}_{t}=r_{t},\label{eq:ramsey_Kfoc}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\pi}{\partial L_{t}}=AK^{\alpha}_{t}(1-\alpha)L^{-\alpha}_{t}-w_{t}=0\iff(1-\alpha)Ak^{\alpha}_{t}=w_{t}.\label{eq:ramsey_Lfoc}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The labor-income share is then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{w_{t}L_{t}}{AK^{\alpha}_{t}L^{1-\alpha}_{t}}=\frac{w_{t}}{Ak^{\alpha}_{t}}=\frac{(1-\alpha)Ak^{\alpha}_{t}}{Ak^{\alpha}_{t}}=1-\alpha.\label{eq:laborincshare}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Empirical labor-income
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The labour-income share for Poland and Belgium for the period 1995-2021 is graphed in figure 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{fig:labincome}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The average for Belgium is 0.564 and for Poland 0.443.
 In the considered model this pins down 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 through 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:laborincshare}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0.564=1-\alpha^{BE}\iff\alpha^{BE}=0.436,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0.443=1-\alpha^{PL}\iff\alpha^{PL}=0.557.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

