\begin_layout Subsection
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Households solve the following utility maximisation problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\max_{\{c_{t},a_{t+1}\}^{\infty}_{t=0}}U & =\sum^{\infty}_{t=0}\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}\frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t}}{1-\sigma}\\
 & s.t.\\
(1+n)a_{t+1} & =a_{t}R_{t}+w_{t}-c_{t}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma>0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\neq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}\equiv1+r_{t}-\delta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}=k_{0}>0$
\end_inset

 given.
 The problem has the following Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}=\left[\sum^{\infty}_{t=0}\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}\frac{c^{1-\sigma}_{t}}{1-\sigma}+\lambda_{t}\left(a_{t}R_{t}+w_{t}-c_{t}-(1+n)a_{t+1}\right)\right],
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with the first order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial c_{t}}=\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}c^{-\sigma}_{t}-\lambda_{t}=0\iff\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}c^{-\sigma}_{t}=\lambda_{t},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial a_{t+1}}=-(1+n)\lambda_{t}+\lambda_{t+1}R_{t+1}=0\iff\frac{1}{1+n}\lambda_{t+1}R_{t+1}=\lambda_{t}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
substituting the multipliers for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}=\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}c^{-\sigma}_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t+1}=\beta^{t+1}(1+n)^{t+1}c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 yields the consumption Euler equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1+n}\left(\beta^{t+1}(1+n)^{t+1}c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}\right)R_{t+1} & =\beta^{t}(1+n)^{t}c^{-\sigma}_{t}\iff
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c^{-\sigma}_{t}=\beta\underbrace{(1+r_{t+1}-\delta)}_{R_{t+1}}c^{-\sigma}_{t+1}.\label{eq:euler_ramsey}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Steady state concentration of capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NEEDS WORK:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We impose market clearing conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
a_{t}=k_{t} & \equiv\frac{K_{t}}{L_{t}},\label{eq:assetclear}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{t}=y_{t}\equiv\frac{AK^{\alpha}_{t}L^{1-\alpha}_{t}}{L_{t}}=Ak^{\alpha}_{t},\label{eq:varemkdclear}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\{0,1,\dots\}$
\end_inset

.
 Using
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:assetclear}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:varemkdclear}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 along with the steady state (SS) condition 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=x_{t+1}=x^{ss}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in{c,r,k}$
\end_inset

,
 the household Euler equation 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:euler_ramsey}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the FOC for firm profit optimization 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:ramsey_Kfoc}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 we obtain steady state capital per hours worked:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
(c^{ss})^{-\sigma} & =\beta(1+r^{ss}-\delta)(c^{ss})^{-\sigma}\\
 & =\beta(1+\alpha A\left(k^{ss}\right)^{\alpha-1}-\delta)(c^{ss})^{-\sigma}\iff\\
\alpha A\left(k^{ss}\right)^{\alpha-1} & =\frac{1}{\beta}+\delta-1\iff\\
k^{ss} & =\left(\frac{\alpha A}{\frac{1}{\beta}+\delta-1}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From here we use that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=Ak^{\alpha}_{t}$
\end_inset

 as shown in 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:varemkdclear}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and calculate the income capital ratio:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z^{ss}\equiv\frac{K^{ss}}{Y^{ss}}=\frac{k^{ss}}{y^{ss}}=\frac{k^{ss}}{A\left(k^{ss}\right)^{\alpha}}=\left(k^{ss}\right)^{1-\alpha}A^{-1}=\left(\left(\frac{\alpha A}{\frac{1}{\beta}+\delta-1}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\alpha}}\right)^{1-\alpha}A^{-1}=\frac{\alpha}{\frac{1}{\beta}+\delta-1}\label{eq:capoutputratio}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Capital-output ratio
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The average capital-output ratio is $z^{BE}=3.410$ for Belgium and $z^{PL}=1.576$ for Poland.
 Assuming both economies are in SS,
 we consider two cases.
 Firstly,
 if depreciation rates are identical,
 we obtain 
\backslash
[
\backslash
frac{
\backslash
beta^{BE}}{
\backslash
beta^{PL}}=
\backslash
frac{1-
\backslash
delta+0.557/1.576}{1-
\backslash
delta+0.436/3.410}>1.
\backslash
] That is,
 Belgian households must be more patient and therefore more willing to postpone consumption.
 For a common annual depreciation rate of $
\backslash
delta=0.05$,
 we obtain 
\backslash
[
\backslash
beta^{BE}=
\backslash
frac{1}{1-0.05+0.436/3.410}
\backslash
approx0.928,
\backslash
qquad
\backslash
beta^{PL}=
\backslash
frac{1}{1-0.05+0.557/1.576}
\backslash
approx0.767.
\backslash
] Secondly,
 if discount factors are identical,
 we obtain 
\backslash
[
\backslash
delta^{PL}-
\backslash
delta^{BE}=
\backslash
frac{0.557}{1.576}-
\backslash
frac{0.436}{3.410}
\backslash
approx0.226.
\backslash
] Poland's annual depreciation rate must therefore be approximately $22.6$ percentage points higher.
 These are substantial differences requiring further justification,
 although differences in capital composition could explain part of the depreciation gap.
 Note also that the model assumes closed economies in SS,
 so these implications need not hold in a richer open-economy framework.
\end_layout

\end_inset


